  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Strong on boundary clarity: payload meaning lives behind `pcid` dispatch and the spec explicitly pushes ordering, forwarding, and body-reference semantics out of the envelope, which helps avoid backdooring a preferred grid-envelope winner. But it does not prove that differently wrapped group-session messages stay equivalently authenticated, because proofs bind outer canonical bytes and no cross-variant equivalence rule is defined.",
    "strengths": [
      "Keeps session meaning in the payload protocol rather than the envelope winner.",
      "Explicit layering rules push ordering, forwarding, and body references into wrappers or payload-specific logic.",
      "Mandatory proof slots give peers locally recordable evidence without a central registry assumption."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "No envelope-neutral rule says when two differently wrapped messages are the same group-session event.",
      "Proofs bind outer canonical bytes, so envelope changes can change verifiability even when payload meaning is unchanged.",
      "Long-term audit still depends on durable access to payload and proof handlers."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Implementers may accidentally let outer-envelope verification become the authority for session semantics.",
      "Best-effort inspection of unknown payloads can be over-trusted by tooling.",
      "Draft-status pCID and proof evolution could cause replay or audit drift over time."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What inner object should group-session logic sign or hash so envelope swaps stay semantically neutral?",
      "How should peers compare two envelope variants carrying the same resolved payload?",
      "What is the required fallback when a future auditor cannot resolve `sig_pcid` or `pcid`?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
